From 49da936eebb2983b2d0de90314c7dd6bd758b560 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sqz kernel recipe <sqz-kernel@squeezefs>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 11:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 31/31] sqz: fuse-uring per-queue background accounting
 (COMMIT-lock split)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The finding (SqueezeFS e2e perf audit, R-4 reap-thread economy,
.benchmarks/2026-09-03-r4-reap-thread-economy.md §2, field 6.19.14-sqz,
kern rand-4k 24 jobs x qd8, FUSE-over-io_uring 32 x 32, zc + kmbuf): the
queue worker (fuse3-ur*) costs 17.7 us per 4 KiB random read, 63 % of it
in the kernel, and 1.9 us of that is pure spinlock contention -
native_queued_spin_lock_slowpath 7.8 % + _raw_spin_lock 3.1 % of the
worker's cycles, callers fuse_uring_req_end (3.8 %, the ring queue's lock
+ the connection's bg_lock nested in it) and fuse_request_end (3.1 %, the
connection's bg_lock again on 6.19; on this tree the two are one
acquisition through fuse_request_bg_finish(), nested in queue->lock)
under fuse_uring_commit_fetch <- io_uring_cmd <- io_submit_sqes.  Every
COMMIT_AND_FETCH ends a background request under a lock that the 24
submitting fio threads (fuse_uring_queue_bq_req) and the other 31 workers
also take: struct fuse_conn owns ONE bg_lock for max_background /
num_background / active_background / blocked, and the ring's per-CPU
queues all share it.  The board item this discharges: "per-CPU/per-queue
background accounting in patches - expected -1.5...-2 us/op on the
worker and on every fio thread's submit path".

Mechanism.  Each struct fuse_ring_queue gets its own background ledger
under the queue->lock it already holds on both paths:

  num_background     requests charged to this queue (FR_BG_URING)
  active_background  the subset moved fuse_req_bg_queue -> fuse_req_queue
  bg_blocked         this queue's admission gate
  bg_waitq           this queue's exclusive waiters

and a per-queue budget fuse_uring_bg_max() = its share of the connection's
max_background: max_background / nr_queues, the remainder to the lowest
qids (the shares sum to max_background exactly whenever it is >=
nr_queues), never below 1 (the historic "one background request per
queue" floor).  It is derived at each admission from a lockless
READ_ONCE - max_background only moves through fusectl and abort
(UINT_MAX) - so the hot paths carry no cache of it.

  * fuse_uring_queue_bq_req(): charge the QUEUE (num_background++,
    bg_blocked = num >= bg_max), flush against the queue's own budget.
    No fc->bg_lock.
  * fuse_uring_flush_bg(): admits while active_background < bg_max.  The
    lockdep_assert_held(&fc->bg_lock) is gone with the lock.
  * fuse_uring_req_end(): fuse_uring_bg_finish() - uncharge, re-open the
    gate when num < bg_max, wake ONE exclusive waiter (the classical
    wake chain, verbatim, with its waitqueue_active() argument), flush.
    No fc->bg_lock.
  * fuse_get_req(): the connection-level gates (INIT, ring readiness,
    the classical fc->blocked) stay on blocked_waitq; once the ring is
    ready a background allocation then waits on ITS queue's gate
    (fuse_uring_bg_wait(), the queue fuse_uring_task_to_queue() names -
    the queue the submit would land on).  fc->blocked governs the
    classical path only; the uring paths never read or write it.
  * The congestion checks (file.c readahead / writepages) read
    fuse_num_background() = the classical ledger + a lockless sum of
    the queues - exact, and off the request path.  fusectl's
    max_background read is unchanged (the limit, not the count).
  * fusectl's max_background write (control.c) re-gates every queue
    against its new share outside bg_lock (fuse_uring_bg_limit_changed()).
  * The classical /dev/fuse path (no ring) keeps the connection-wide
    accounting verbatim; fuse_request_bg_finish() becomes static to it.
  * No uapi change.  No Kconfig change.

Correctness, preserved and reasoned:

  (1) No lost wakeups.  Waiters sleep exclusively on the queue's
      bg_waitq; every gate transition set->cleared happens under
      queue->lock and is followed by a full wake_up(), and the
      not-blocked arm's waitqueue_active() wake is safe for exactly the
      reason the classical code gives: a waiter only sleeps after seeing
      the gate set, so any waiter the transition's wake_up() did not see
      observes the clear instead.  One new hazard is closed explicitly:
      a woken waiter is placed by the CPU it runs on and may resubmit on
      ANOTHER queue, so a queue's wake chain can run dry with waiters
      left behind - the last charged request out of a queue wakes them
      all (fuse_uring_bg_finish()'s drain-time wake_up_all()).  A slot a
      woken task does not use (allocation failure, request dropped
      unsent) is passed on (fuse_uring_bg_kick()) like before.
  (2) Abort with requests queued per queue.  fuse_abort_conn() raises
      max_background to UINT_MAX (now WRITE_ONCE), so
      fuse_uring_abort_end_requests()'s flush moves every queued
      background request to fuse_req_queue; the gate is opened and the
      waiters released under the same lock.  Every path that ends a
      charged request WITHOUT fuse_uring_req_end() -
      fuse_uring_abort_end_queue_requests() (fuse_req_queue ->
      fuse_dev_end_requests()) and fuse_uring_entry_teardown() (requests
      still attached to ents; it list_del_init()s ent->fuse_req under
      queue->lock, the credit sits beside it) - credits the queue under
      queue->lock first (fuse_uring_bg_uncharge()), so num_background
      and active_background return to zero and the connection ledger,
      which was never charged for them, is never debited (the underflow
      the naive split would have introduced).
  (3) FR_BACKGROUND clear vs fuse_request_end()'s re-check.  The
      per-queue credit clears FR_BACKGROUND under queue->lock in the
      same task, before fuse_request_end(req) runs, so fuse_request_end()
      never takes bg_lock for a queue-charged request - the same order
      the fuse_request_bg_finish() split used.
      FR_BG_URING (a new internal request flag) marks the ledger a
      request was charged to: a background request the classical
      flush_bg_queue() hands to the ring across the fiq->ops switch-over
      (FR_BACKGROUND without FR_BG_URING) stays on the connection's
      ledger and is credited by fuse_request_end() exactly as before -
      the one remaining bg_lock take on a uring end, only for that
      transitional class.  (Before this patch that class also
      decremented queue->active_background it never incremented.)
  (4) max_background shrink at runtime.  fuse_uring_bg_limit_changed()
      closes the gates of queues now over budget; they re-open as their
      requests end.  Only charged requests are ever credited, so nothing
      underflows; queued background requests admit only when
      active_background < the new share, so the queue drains to it.  A
      grow opens gates and hands one slot on per queue, like fusectl's
      classical write.
  (5) Semantics, stated plainly.  A queue whose share is exhausted
      blocks ITS submitters even while other queues have room - the
      original's "one bg request per queue, ignoring global limits"
      locality made general.  For SqueezeFS's INIT reply
      (max_background = clamp(queues x q_depth, 64, u16::MAX), the
      delivered ring capacity) a queue's share is exactly its ent count,
      so the only behavior change on that daemon is the locking.  A
      daemon whose max_background is below nr_queues gets one slot per
      queue (nr_queues in flight, not max_background) - the floor the
      flush already granted to active_background, now also to admission.

Lock order: queue->lock alone on both hot paths.  fuse_uring_bg_limit_changed()
takes queue->lock after control.c dropped bg_lock, so no path nests
queue->lock inside bg_lock (the historic nesting was the reverse).
Compile-proven with CONFIG_PROVE_LOCKING=y + CONFIG_DEBUG_SPINLOCK=y;
runtime lockdep is the boot test's.

The A/B row: same daemon, kernel A (sqz series 0001-0030) vs kernel B
(+0031), field kern rand-4k 24 x qd8 30 s + the 1 MiB seq rows, A A B B
across reboots with the box's thermal state recorded; columns
daemon_cpu_ns_by_class[fuse3-ur] per op, read_transport_phase_ns, the
perf ledger's native_queued_spin_lock_slowpath share on the workers, IOPS,
clat p50/p99 (.benchmarks/rigs/2026-09-06-kernel-bg-per-queue-ab.sh).
Expected on the ledger: -1.5...-2 us/op on the worker and on every
submitter.  Not boot-tested here; the lever holds its slot on that row.

Design note: docs/design-kernel-bg-per-queue.md (§6 is this backport's
ledger).

[sqz 7.1.6 backport] Authored on 7.2.3 as 0026 (fuse_chan split);
backported per the design note's §6 ledger: struct fuse_conn owns the
accounting (fc-> for fch->, ring->fc for ring->chan, fuse_abort_conn()
for fuse_chan_abort()); fuse_request_bg_finish(fc, req) exists on this
tree and goes static (its declaration leaves fuse_dev_i.h); no
fuse_chan_{num,max}_background accessors - file.c's two congestion checks
read the new fuse_num_background() inline helper (dev_uring_i.h, both
CONFIG arms) and control.c's inline max_background write gains the
WRITE_ONCE + the re-gate hook (file.c/control.c gain #include
"dev_uring_i.h" like inode.c); fuse_block_alloc() is one expression (the
!fuse_uring_ready(fc) clause added to its blocked arm); fuse_uring_abort()
already walks the queues unconditionally, so the abort arm is 0026's.
Nothing in the patch is gcc-8.5-hostile (no cleanup attributes, no
__auto_type, no C23).
